{"id":"nasa-ugdl8r","src":"nasa","t":"APOD: 2026 November 10 – Saturn Behind the Moon","u":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-10-saturn-behind-the-moon/","at":"2026-11-10T05:05:00.000Z","by":"","img":null,"body":[{"p":["APOD APOD"]},{"p":["Astronomy Picture of the Day"]},{"p":["Discover the cosmos! Each day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is featured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer."]},{"img":{"src":"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Moonsaturn6_Ortu_2124.jpg?w=2048&h=1525&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint","cap":"The curving surface of the Moon occupies the lower half of the image. Just above center the planet Saturn appears, far in the distance."}},{"h":"Saturn Behind the Moon"},{"p":[{"b":"Explanation:"}," Sometimes Saturn disappears. It doesn’t really go away, of course — it just ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-april-29-saturns-rings-appear-to-disappear/","t":"disappears"}," from our view when ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2021-september-5-earth-and-moon/","t":"Earth’s Moon"}," moves in front. Such a ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/","t":"Saturn"},"ian eclipse, more formally called an ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-28-the-mutual-occultation-of-ganymede-and-io/","t":"occultation"},", was visible in early 2025 along a small ",{"a":"https://in-the-sky.org/news/occultations/occultation_20250104_1652_visibility.png","t":"swath"}," of ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/facts/","t":"Earth"}," — from ",{"a":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenland","t":"Greenland"}," through central ",{"a":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Europe","t":"Europe"}," and into northern ",{"a":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Africa","t":"Africa"},". The ",{"a":"https://www.flickr.com/photos/194524290@N05/54963364007/","t":"featured image"}," is a digital fusion of three images, the first of just ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod/apod-2026-august-23-cassini-approaches-saturn/","t":"Saturn"}," before it disappeared, and then two more of the Moon with ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2020-august-24-crescent-moon-hdr/","t":"different exposure"}," lengths. The images were taken from ",{"a":"https://youtu.be/z6hf3TY5LZU","t":"Sardinia"},", ",{"a":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italy","t":"Italy"},", after sunset. The bright dot near Saturn is not a ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod/apod-2026-august-11-six-moons-of-saturn/","t":"moon of Saturn"},", but rather the star ",{"a":"https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/85_Aquarii","t":"85 Aquarii"},", which is further behind Saturn than Saturn is behind the Moon. The next time the ",{"a":"https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/5587/","t":"Moon"}," moves in front of Saturn, as visible from ",{"a":"https://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcRI9kyfDjt8-b4RVVADhbiFfBl5MR8Rl16peGoUYAuP0PwITdzj7rYB1EnN","t":"some place on Earth"},", will be in 2031."]},{"p":[{"b":"Tomorrow’s picture:"},"small but mighty"]},{"more":true},{"p":[{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/random-apod/","t":"Random APOD Generator"},{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-9-rigel-and-the-witch-head-nebula/","t":"Yesterday’s Image APOD: 2026 November 9 – Rigel and the Witch Head Nebula"},"Tomorrow’s Image"]}]}
